 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "Single-patient case report of a woman with an LCAT variant: 'Genetic testing ... was positive for LCAT c.490 C>T (p.R164C) as the likely pathogenic variant, confirming the diagnosis of LCAT deficiency.'",
 "method_note": "Read by hand; JSON assembled with a short Python script in scratch, all values from my reading. One person (unnamed patient of a case report, label 'patient'; adopted, unknown family history, so no relatives). Age 34 is the age at presentation/referral; recorded as age at last evaluation because no other age is given, but she is followed annually afterwards and no separate age at diagnosis is printed (diagnosis followed the same work-up). Zygosity not stated: only one LCAT variant is printed and the paper calls LCAT deficiency autosomal recessive in general, so zygosity left 'not reported'; inheritance not reported (adopted). Variant type not stated as a class (only 'C>T mutation', arginine to cysteine), so 'not reported'. The Discussion prints the protein change as 'p.R.164 C' in addition to 'p.R164C'. Low apoA1 and apoB are read from Table 1 values below the printed reference ranges; triglycerides (164 mg/dl), LDL (68 mg/dl) and total cholesterol have no printed range, so hypertriglyceridemia is not reported and low LDL is not claimed from LDL itself (checklist 'low ldl cholesterol or apolipoprotein b' observed via apoB). 'Dilated fundus examination was normal' is a normal examination as a whole and was recorded as the paper words it (abnormal fundus examination, excluded). Normal serum creatinine excludes elevated creatinine only, not chronic kidney disease. No renal biopsy reported. Disease features in the Discussion are general statements, not used for this person. No contradictions found.",
